LICENSE — NOT YET CHOSEN
========================

Porpoise Robotics has not selected a license for this repository yet. This file is a
placeholder so that the decision does not get forgotten.

WHAT THIS MEANS RIGHT NOW
-------------------------

Under copyright law, code with no license is "all rights reserved" by default. Making the
repository public lets people read it, but does not give anyone permission to use, copy,
modify, or redistribute it. If the goal is for students, schools, or other educators to be
able to build on this work, a license has to be added explicitly.

WHO DECIDES
-----------

This is a decision for Porpoise Robotics leadership, not for individual contributors. It has
legal and organizational consequences, so it should not be picked by whoever happens to be
committing that day.

SOME COMMON OPTIONS
-------------------

  MIT             Very permissive and very short. Anyone may use the code for anything,
                  including commercially, as long as they keep the copyright notice.
                  The most common choice for educational and hobby robotics projects.

  Apache-2.0      Permissive like MIT, but also includes an explicit patent grant and
                  requires changes to be noted. Preferred by many organizations that want
                  clearer legal protection.

  GPL-3.0         Copyleft. Anyone may use and modify the code, but if they distribute a
                  modified version they must also release their source code under the GPL.
                  Choose this if the organization wants derivative work to stay open.

  CC BY-SA 4.0    Designed for documentation, curriculum, and other written material rather
                  than for software. Sometimes used alongside a software license when a
                  repository contains a lot of teaching content.

https://choosealicense.com/ walks through the tradeoffs in plain language and is a good
starting point for the conversation.

HOW TO ADD ONE
--------------

Once the organization decides, replace this entire file with the full text of the chosen
license, fill in the copyright year and holder (for example: "Copyright (c) 2026 Porpoise
Robotics"), and mention the choice in README.md. GitHub can also insert the standard text for
you: in the repository, choose Add file > Create new file, type "LICENSE" as the filename, and
a "Choose a license template" button appears.
